---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudyHeader from '../../components/notebook/CaseStudyHeader.astro';
import CaseHero from '../../components/notebook/CaseHero.astro';
import SectionRow from '../../components/notebook/SectionRow.astro';
---

<Base title="Sous · VK">
  <div style="background: var(--paper); min-height: 100vh; color: var(--ink); padding-bottom: 120px">

    <CaseStudyHeader entryNum="" />

    <CaseHero
      eyebrow="Sous · CMU Human-AI Interaction"
      title="Your second set of eyes in the kitchen"
      lede="A hands-free AI cooking companion that watches your pan through a camera, tracks where you are in the recipe, and speaks up only when it's useful. In progress — final presentation early December 2026."
      meta={[
        { label: "Status", value: "In progress — final due Dec 2026" },
        { label: "Team", value: "Andrea, Rachit, Varrshitha, Dora" },
        { label: "Context", value: "CMU Human-AI Interaction, Fall 2026" },
        { label: "Stack", value: "Python, Amazon Bedrock, vision-language model, voice controls" },
      ]}
      note="the brief: help home cooks follow recipes hands-free.<br><br>the hard part: knowing when to advance and when to stay quiet."
    />

    <SectionRow num="01" label="the problem">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          Timers tell you when time is up, not whether the onion is actually translucent. Recipe
          apps show the next step, but they don't know you're still on the last one. When something
          goes differently than the recipe expects, you're on your own.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          Sous watches the pan. A camera sends snapshots to a vision-language model every few
          seconds, the model compares what it sees to the expected state of the current step, and
          Sous either advances, waits, or asks. Voice controls keep your hands free. Manual override
          keeps you in charge.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(-1deg)">
          we started with local vision models (Qwen3-VL 8B). they took 10–30 seconds per response.
          moved to Amazon Bedrock. cloud is faster; latency is still the constraint.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <SectionRow num="02" label="the core design problem">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          When should Sous move on? Too early and it's wrong; too late and it's useless. The answer
          we landed on: two consecutive confident observations at 85% or above before auto-advancing.
          One confident look could be a fluke. Two in a row is a pattern.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          Sous also shows you its current belief: what it thinks it sees, what it's waiting for.
          If it's wrong, you correct it and move on. The design principle it comes from is the same
          as WorkBench HR: AI makes a suggestion, the person decides. "Wrong call? You're still
          the chef."
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(0.6deg)">
          the question the class asked that stuck: when the model is unsure, should Sous ask, wait
          silently, or make a best guess? we chose to wait and show the uncertainty. a wrong guess
          in a kitchen is worse than a pause.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <SectionRow num="03" label="where it is now" pb="104px">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          The MVP checkpoint covered recipe selection, step tracking with manual override, timer
          controls, and the camera panel. Action detection works for simple actions; more complex
          state recognition is the next problem. User testing in a real kitchen is mid-November.
          This page will update after the final presentation in December.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(-0.6deg)">
          same question as Halo: when does the AI interrupt, and when does staying quiet serve the
          user better? different domain, same design tension.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <footer class="cs-row" style="padding-top: 24px; align-items: end">
      <div></div>
      <a href="/projects" class="back mono">← Projects</a>
      <div></div>
    </footer>

  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .body {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 21px;
    line-height: 1.62;
    margin: 0 0 22px;
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 20, 'wght' 400;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .back { color: var(--ink-60); text-decoration: none; font-size: 13px; }
  .back:hover { color: var(--ink); }
  @media (max-width: 1000px) { .body { font-size: 19px; } }
</style>
